# Frontier-CS (algorithmic track) defaults, sourced by run.sh.

# The dataset is a local directory generated by setup.sh from the vendored
# problem tree, passed to harbor via -p.
DATASET_SUBDIR="datasets/frontier-cs-algorithm"

# The problem tree the go-judge reads test data from, relative to eval/. Exported
# by run.sh as FRONTIER_CS_ALGORITHMIC_PATH, which the generated
# docker-compose.yaml interpolates when it bind-mounts one problem into the judge
# container.
ALGORITHMIC_SUBDIR="frontier-cs/algorithmic"

# harbor imports the agent by this module:class path. The directory CONTAINING the
# package goes on PYTHONPATH.
AGENT_IMPORT_PATH="terminus_2.terminus_2:Terminus2"

# The local image tags setup.sh builds/pulls. MAIN_IMAGE and JUDGE_IMAGE are written
# into every generated task, so harbor never builds a per-task image and the judge
# never reaches a registry at trial time. PROXY_IMAGE is named by the generated
# compose file (it is the judge proxy's base) and is pre-pulled for the same reason.
MAIN_IMAGE="frontier-cs-main:latest"
JUDGE_IMAGE="frontier-cs-judge:latest"
PROXY_IMAGE="python:3.12-slim"

# --- Fixed: the model capability ---------------------------------------------
# These decide how much inference the agent buys per attempt. They are held
# constant so a change in score means the agent got better rather than bigger.
# run.sh passes them to harbor itself, and refuses to evaluate an edit that has
# changed a line setting one of them. See ENVIRONMENT.md.
#
# Model the terminus-2 agent runs on. Bedrock via LiteLLM. Use the BARE id —
# a "[1m]" suffix is a Claude Code alias and is not valid on Bedrock's
# InvokeModel API.
DEFAULT_MODEL="bedrock/global.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8"
DEFAULT_TEMPERATURE="1.0"
# terminus-2 leaves both of these unset, and they stay unset: setting either buys
# extended thinking the shipped agent does not have. Empty means "do not pass it",
# which is how the agent already runs.
DEFAULT_REASONING_EFFORT=""
DEFAULT_MAX_THINKING_TOKENS=""

# The wall clock one attempt gets inside the container, as a multiplier on the
# task's own agent timeout. Frontier-CS ships 10800s (three hours) per attempt, and
# terminus-2's episode cap is effectively unbounded (1000000), so time is the only
# thing that limits how many model calls one attempt makes. That makes it a
# capability knob, not a convenience: 0.25 fixes it at 2700s so an agent cannot
# raise its score by grinding for longer. It is not a flag, and run.sh refuses an
# edit that reaches the same thing from inside the agent.
AGENT_TIMEOUT_MULT="0.25"

# Multiplier on harbor's container build/start window. Results-neutral: the agent's
# solve budget and the verifier are untouched. Widened because a cold-start burst
# under concurrency otherwise trips the default and produces phantom zeros.
ENV_BUILD_TIMEOUT_MULT="3.0"

# Multiplier on the verifier (judge) window. Left at 1.0: the adapter already sizes
# it per problem from case count and time limit, and shrinking it would score a
# correct solution zero on the slowest problems — a failure about the harness, not
# the agent.
VERIFIER_TIMEOUT_MULT="1.0"

# Names that decide capability rather than behaviour. run.sh refuses to evaluate an
# edit that touches a line mentioning one, so a capability purchase cannot be
# mistaken for an improvement. max_turns/max_episodes are here because they are the
# call-count cap: an unguarded step cap is a way to buy inference without touching
# the model. Extend if terminus-2 grows another.
CAPABILITY_KNOBS="reasoning_effort|max_thinking_tokens|temperature|model_name|llm_backend|interleaved_thinking|use_responses_api|max_turns|max_episodes|override_timeout_sec|max_timeout_sec|agent_timeout"

# Names that would let the agent read the answer key instead of solving the
# problem. The judge grades inside its own container, but its test data is
# bind-mounted from a directory in this run, and the agent's control loop is a host
# process that can read it. Nothing stops that at the filesystem level, so it is
# checked the same way the capability is: run.sh refuses to evaluate an edit that
# introduces a line mentioning one of these. None of them appear in the agent as
# shipped.
#
# These are host paths, not words. The list deliberately does not contain bare
# "testdata": a prompt template that tells the model to write its own test data is a
# legitimate edit and would otherwise be refused. It is a tripwire on the obvious
# route, not a proof that the answer key was not read.
INTEGRITY_REFS="FRONTIER_CS_ALGORITHMIC_PATH|frontier-cs/algorithmic|algorithmic/problems|/testdata|datasets/frontier-cs-algorithm"

# --- Excluded from every run -------------------------------------------------
# Failure modes that are about the task rather than the agent go here, and are
# removed from every evaluation.
#
# Empty. Every one of the 188 problems was checked against the judge's own loader
# before this set was written: each has a testdata/ directory, each declares a
# checker/interactor file that exists, and no problem declares a type the judge
# does not implement. Nothing was excluded on evidence, and nothing should be
# excluded without it — a task removed because the agent scores badly on it is the
# task set being fitted to the agent.
ALWAYS_EXCLUDE=""

# --- The task set ------------------------------------------------------------
# ALL 188 problems of the Frontier-CS algorithmic track, as the upstream repo
# publishes them — enumerated here so a run directory is standalone and says
# exactly what it was measured on. Upstream problem ids are sparse (they run to
# 315 with gaps), so the count and the largest id do not agree; the list is the
# authority, not a range.
#
# One set, all of it visible and all of it measured: there is no held-out split.
# `--tasks` picks from this set and `--attempts` repeats it.
#
# 114 problems are standard (stdin/stdout) and 74 are interactive (the solution
# talks to a hidden interactor over a pipe). 121 carry a custom checker, which is
# what makes the reward continuous rather than pass/fail.
